@page "/"
@using MudBlazor

<PageTitle>Home</PageTitle>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h3" Class="mb-2">Library App — Take-Home Assignment</MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-6">
    I haven't beeen to a library in a while so there were a few gaps in my understanding of how libraries work
    that I didn't realise until it was a bit too late haha. Like for example I coded it such that you cant return
    a book until you've paid the late fee, but only when I was doing the delpoyment that I realised 'wait wouldnt
    they want you to return it then just hound you for the fee later'. Anyway on this page you'll find a brief explanation
    about the application.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-6">
    This solution noticeably does not have any Controllers. That's because the UI is built with Blazor Server, which 
    is a Microsoft web UI framework where a page's rendering logic runs server side, and UI updates are pushed using websockets
    instead of shipping a separate JavaScript single-page app that talks to a JSON API. So the C# code-behind of the 'frontend' 
    calls the Application layer's MediatR handlers directly. &nbsp;
    If you needed a real FE framework not much would have to change - Just need to port the frontend stuff to whatever FE framework
    and then write up controllers that call the CQRS handlers through network calls.
</MudText>

<MudDivider Class="mb-6"/>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h4" Class="mb- ">Architecture: Clean Architecture + DDD</MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
    The solution is split into three projects, each a layer, with dependencies only pointing inward:
</MudText>
<MudPaper Class="pa-4 mb-3" Elevation="1">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Library.Domain</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
        Plain C#, no framework or infrastructure dependencies. Business rules (like "you cant remove a book that 
        still has copies on loan") are encapsulated in domain aggregates, with their own invariants and repository interfaces.
    </MudText>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Book</b> (aggregate root) owns its <b>BookCopy</b> entities. Copies can only be added/removed
            through the book (<code>AddCopy()</code>, <code>RemoveCopy()</code>), and removal is refused unless
            a copy is currently <code>Available</code> — you can't yank a book out from under someone who has it
            on loan.
        </li>
        <li><b>Loan</b> encapsulates checkout → due date → return/overdue → late-fee calculation. A loan
            snapshots its loan period and fee-per-day <i>at checkout time</i> from whatever the latest
            <b>LoanPolicy</b> was, rather than pointing at a live policy, so changing the policy later never
            rewrites the terms of a loan already in progress.
        </li>
        <li><b>LoanPolicy</b> is an append-only, versioned record of "loan period + late fee per day, effective
            from a given date" — a simple history rather than a single mutable settings row.
        </li>
        <li><b>Member</b> is its own aggregate root (profile name/email/interests), deliberately <i>not</i>
            holding collections of its Loans/Reservations/Payments. Those are independent aggregates linked only
            by a <code>MemberId</code>, which keeps each aggregate's invariants — and each save transaction —
            small and focused.
        </li>
        <li><b>Reservation</b> has its own lifecycle: <code>Pending → Notified → Fulfilled</code> or
            <code>Cancelled</code>.
        </li>
        <li><b>Payment</b> is a simple ledger entry, optionally tagged with the loan it's paying off.</li>
        <li>A shared <b>Money</b> value object wraps monetary amounts everywhere fees/payments are handled.</li>
    </ul>
</MudPaper>
<MudPaper Class="pa-4 mb-3" Elevation="1">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Library.Application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
        I used CQRS for the app layer of this app. Every use case is a small, single-purpose
        command/query + handler pair — <code>AddBookCommand</code>, <code>CheckoutBookCommand</code>,
        <code>ReturnBookCommand</code>, <code>ReserveBookCommand</code>, <code>RecordPaymentCommand</code>,
        <code>GetMemberDashboardQuery</code>, and so on — that orchestrate the domain aggregates and repositories.
        Interesting side-effects are modelled as domain events rather than hard-coded chains: returning a book
        raises a <code>BookReturnedEvent</code> inside the <code>Loan</code> aggregate, which the handler
        republishes as a <code>BookReturnedNotification</code>; a separate handler
        (<code>NotifyNextReservationHandler</code>) reacts to that notification by flipping every pending
        reservation on that book to <code>Notified</code> — the return flow and the reservation flow never call
        each other directly.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>
<MudPaper Class="pa-4 mb-3" Elevation="1">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Library.Blazor</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
        The composition root and infrastructure layer, and also the UI: a Blazor Server app (interactive server
        render mode) using MudBlazor for components, ASP.NET Core Identity for authentication (cookie-based,
        email + password), and EF Core over SQLite for persistence, with one repository implementation per
        domain repository interface. Pages talk to the Application layer purely through
        <code>IMediator.Send(...)</code> — there's no separate REST/HTTP API sitting between "frontend" and
        "backend" here; see the assumptions below for why that's fine for this exercise.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
        Actually if I could've I would've moved the Entity Framework Core (the ORM of .NET) stuff into a seperate project
        called Library.EfCore or something but the solution template came with the files already pre created and it would've
        been a pain to move everything so I left them in the /Data of Library.Blazor. But the real clean architecture would decouple
        this layer. But for simplicity and to quickly get this small project out I just left it.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudDivider Class="mb-6"/>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h4" Class="mb-3">Authentication &amp; identity</MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
    I used the built in <b>ASP.NET Core Identity</b> for auth registered via <code>AddIdentityCore&lt;ApplicationUser&gt;()</code> 
    with EF Core as its storage provider. That means users, roles, and password hashes live in the same SQLite database 
    as everything else, in Identity's standard <code>AspNetUsers</code>/<code>AspNetRoles</code> tables, and the 
    self-service registration, login, email-confirmation all comes with it built in.
</MudText>

<MudDivider Class="mb-6"/>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h4" Class="mb-3">Pages &amp; features</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mt-4 mb-2">Catalogue — <code>/catalogue</code></MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
    Open to everyone, including anonymous visitors. A search box filters the list by title.
    Signed-in members see a <b>Borrow</b> button when a copy is free, or a <b>Reserve</b> button when every copy
    is currently on loan. Admins additionally get an inline "Add a Book" form and, per row, <b>Add Copy</b>,
    <b>Remove Copy</b> (refused if no copy of that title is currently available), and <b>Delete Book</b>
    (refused if any copy is on loan) — this is book catalogue management: add/remove books and copies, with
    guardrails so you can't delete stock that's actually out with a borrower.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mt-4 mb-2">My Loans — <code>/my-loans</code></MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
    Lists everything you've borrowed: checkout date, due date, a status chip (Active / Overdue / Returned),
    the late fee calculated live as of today, and the outstanding fee after subtracting any payments already
    made. <b>Return</b> hands the book back — but is refused until any owed late fee is paid in full first, via
    the <b>Pay Fee</b> button. Symmetrically, <b>Borrow</b>/checkout on the Catalogue page is refused while you
    have <i>any</i> active loan with an unpaid fee — so a fee follows you around until it's settled, but only
    blocks you at the two natural chokepoints (borrowing more, or giving that specific book back). This is the
    lending/returning/late-fee/day-tracking use case.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mt-4 mb-2">My Reservations — <code>/my-reservations</code></MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
    Reserving a title is only offered when it has zero available copies. A reservation starts
    <code>Pending</code>. The moment <i>any</i> copy of that book is returned, every pending reservation on that
    title flips to <code>Notified</code> at once — shown here as a green status chip. From this page you can
    <b>Fulfil Reservation</b> (completes the checkout for you, once a copy is actually free) or
    <b>Cancel Reservation</b> at any time. Because everyone waiting is notified together rather than in strict
    queue order, whoever clicks Fulfil first gets the copy; this is deliberately the simplest version of
    "reserve + notify" that still demonstrates the full lifecycle — see the limitations below for what a
    production version would add.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mt-4 mb-2">Profile — <code>/profile</code></MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
    Name/email header, an "add interest" box, and a tabbed dashboard — <b>Active Loans</b>, <b>History</b> (past/returned loans), 
    <b>Reservations</b>, <b>Payments</b>, and <b>Interests</b> — all sourced from a single <code>GetMemberDashboardQuery</code> 
    so a member's whole relationship with the library is visible in one place. This is the user-management use case: profile,
    history, interests, and payments together.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mt-4 mb-2">Admin → Loan Policy — <code>/admin/loan-policy</code></MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
    Lets an admin add a new policy version (loan period in days, late fee per day, effective date) and shows the
    full version history below it. Existing loans are unaffected by a new version — only checkouts made after it
    takes effect pick up the new terms.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mt-4 mb-2">Admin → Members — <code>/admin/members</code></MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
    A flat directory of every member's name and email
</MudText>

<MudDivider Class="mb-6 mt-6"/>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h4" Class="mb-3">Assumptions &amp; known limitations</MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
    Here's where i explain the corners i cut/limitations i made for myself and what id do if i had more time (or if ppl were actually paying to use this)
</MudText>
<ol>
    <li class="mb-2">A single Blazor Server app plays both "frontend" and "backend": MediatR command/query
        handlers stand in for a services/API layer, called in-process rather than over HTTP. The assignment asks
        for the concepts of frontend, backend, and database to be present, not necessarily a network boundary
        between the first two — so I kept it to one deployable for simplicity.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">Reserving is only possible once a title has zero available copies — you can't "reserve"
        something you could just borrow outright.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">Returning a book is blocked until any late fee on it is paid in full; checking out a new
        book is blocked while any active loan has an unpaid fee. If I could do it again i would make it such that
        you can return the book, but you need to pay off the loan before borrowing more.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">Overdue status is computed lazily, refreshed whenever a member's loans are loaded (e.g. on
        My Loans or the Profile dashboard), rather than by a background job sweeping the database on a timer.
        On a real project Id have some sort of background job that periodically updates the statuses
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">"Notification when available" is implemented today as a structured server log line plus the
        status chip flipping to <code>Notified</code> on the Reservations/Profile pages — there's no email or
        push notification wired up. All pending reservers on a title are notified simultaneously rather than
        strictly by queue order, and checkout doesn't reserve the freed copy for the notified member —
        first to click Fulfil wins, and if you lose that race you just wait for the next copy to free up.
        I just did this to demonstrate that for a given event multiple people can receive the notification.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">Demo data (see below) is seeded once at first startup, guarded by "if no books exist yet"
        checks, so restarting the app never duplicates it.
    </li>
</ol>

<MudDivider Class="mb-6 mt-6"/>

